Jude Shin

Notes for Grading: All steps of this project were completed.

The setting that I chose for my scene is a camping scene. I like camping and 
nature; I also like to rock climb, so a lot of the times I would go camping in 
very mountainous regions. Some of my favorite places to climb are Yosemite, CA, 
Mammoth, CA, and Kings Canyon, CA.

You will see a mountain environment (one mesh), three trees (two meshes each; 
branch and leaf clusters), two dark clouds (20+ "fluff" meshes), one tent 
(one mesh), and one dancing flower (three meshes).

The main animation is in the rotation and scaling of the dancing flower. The 
petals oscillate around the center of the petal, and the leaves/stem scale 
thin and thick. The trees are also animated more subtly: the branches rotate
left and right slightly, and each one of the leaf clusters on the trees 
translate from their original positions by a different constant delta.

The hardest part of this assignment was getting the petals centered so the 
rotation would look "normal". I had to translate and rotate it so that the 
petals were in line with one of the axis (at 0, 0, 0), and only then could I 
rotate it based on an animation delta. After that, then I could reverse the 
steps to put the petals back into the right location in the original object. 
Scaling was done on all the parts of the flower (not just one).

The two dark clouds use the solid color shader, whereas the rest of the objects 
use the rainbow colors to show depth and detail.

When I was searching for the obj file, all of the good ones were not free. I 
think a lot of them were also used as assets for game development, so I saw a 
lot of "themed packs" where it could include everything from a rock to a tree 
and a cloud. It was the gaming demographic and the 3d printing demographic that 
I seemed to encounter the most. Not a lot of real world representations or 
moc-ups. I tried to find Yosemite Valley, but all the ones that were free 
didn't look right (they were also meant to be 3D printed).

The A and D keys work for rotating about the flower (as I thought it was the 
most important element); however if you rotate too far you will clip into the 
underside of the mountain because it is super big. It looks weird, but it is 
to be expected.

